 side of broccoli  with some steak the carbohydrates you  ate are first broken down into simple  sugars these simple sugars are first  metabolized into glucose and then  converted into glycogen glycogen is just  a long chain of glucose molecules and  its structure makes it easier for the  body to store the body stores glycogen  primarily in two places eighty percent  in your skeletal muscles which is about  400 grams of glycogen and the remaining  20 percent is stored in your liver or  about 100 grams your skeletal muscles  need their own glycogen stores because  they perform high energy tasks like  walking running lifting and moving heavy  objects in order to do this it needs its  own on-the-spot fuel source in high  quantities oh what's that the biceps  need to curl an 80-pound barbell just  let me break off some glucose from these  glycogen stores just take this right  here  there we are convert it to atp  and voila muscle contraction glycogen is  also stored in the liver to deliver  glucose into the blood to reach the rest  of the body your organs and tissue that  are not skeletal muscle do not have  their own glycogen stores and must get  their glucose from the blood for example  when your intestines need to contract to  move digested food through it it uses  glucose from the blood to power it to  get the glucose there the liver breaks  off glucose from its glycogen stores and  sends it into the blood it makes its way  to the intestines and everyone is happy  especially you if you were constipated  now in the earlier example where your  carb intake was low your glycogen stores  did not get completely filled in terms  of weight loss this is very good the way  you gain weight is by eating too many  carbs so much that your glycogen stores  get completely filled in terms of eating  too many carbs you ate so many that  there's still glucose left over that  cannot fit in the glycogen stores this  glucose has to go somewhere your body  converts it into fatty acids and then  stores them in fat cells over time if  you continue to eat more carbs and your  glycogen stores can hold you'll continue  to allocate excess glucose into fat  cells and gain weight but just how many  grams of carbs per day is overeating  your daily carb intake well on a typical  2000 calories per day diet anything over  325 grams of carbs per day is considered  excessive carb intake but weight gain  from excessive eating is not exclusive  to carbohydrates if you eat too much  protein or fat you will gain weight it's  just that with carbohydrates it's much  easier to overeat when you eat a meal  high in protein or fat you're more  satiated and stop eating much sooner  this is because protein and fat both  higher in calories per gram than  carbohydrates take longer to be  metabolized and both are not primary  fuel sources so your body does not use  them immediately carbohydrates on the  other hand being lower in calories have  an easier digestible molecular structure  and are a primary fuel source so they're  metabolized much faster since  carbohydrates are metabolized at a  faster rate you feel hungry again after  a short period of time thereby making  you seek out more food consuming more  calories and potentially gaining more  weight it's like adding fuel to a fire  carbohydrates are similar to hay they're  light and burn quickly whereas protein  and fat are similar to logs they do have  more fuel but require more time to be  processed they have to be split up and  broken down before they can be put into  the fire which of course takes longer  than burning hay on top of that  ready-to-eat carbs that are disguised as  real food are more available and cheaper  compared to healthy or protein-rich  snacks for example if you're rushing to  work you can grab a muffin a bagel a  danish or a cereal bar even a bowl of  cereal is quick and easy and they're all  100 simple carbohydrates a bacon and egg  sandwich costs more and takes longer to  make than a simple grab-and-go breakfast  carb if you eat out a high carbohydrate  pasta is cheaper than a salmon or steak  dinner oh and don't forget the insane  amount of sugar in fruit and soft drinks  yikes even with snacking you can eat an  entire bag of chips or cookies in two  days but you can eat a whole jar of  peanut butter in the same time trust me  i've tried my point is in our modern  society it's much easier to consume two  to three times the recommended daily  intake of carbs without even trying and  on top of that live a tempting sedentary  lifestyle the most effective way to  avoid weight gain is something you've  heard over and over again diet and  exercise specifically for diet if you  have to eat simple carbohydrates because  you're in a rush or are low on cash  avoid making your diet exclusively based  on cheap simple carbohydrates complex  carbs like whole grains and oats are  high in fiber and take longer to  metabolize so you stay full longer  they're also not that much more  expensive than ready to eat simple carbs  also exercise will increase your  metabolism and reduce some of your  glycogen stores thereby avoiding the  immediate storage of glucose into your  fat cells
 

